## §1 — OBSERVE

- **Queue state (read this cycle):** `hidden_files/study-jobs/QUEUE.md` RECREATION QUEUE — T219 stands at queue #101 (Packet #12), status DESCRIBED-OWED, first attempt owned by this job (A3 §3). The wave RUN.md §0b census concurs (DESCRIBED-OWED #100–#105; #101 is this item).
- **Consumer (named, §1.4):** the **DLC assembler diagram / count-board container** — the block-weighted counting surface owned by the Daily Language Challenge assembler lane (packet §1 consumer text: a count whose unit is a weighted block, state-as-entity, electoral-vote weight-as-increment, running total-as-result; style-library §T219: "Feeds: diagram"). The machinery lifts as a two-tile population board whose payoff is a per-tile square count landing beside each tile, numerals last as verdicts (packet asset AS-98).

### 1.1 Catalogue mechanics (read firsthand)
- Digest §T18: "A person icon carries the 'Three-fifths clause' label; then the payoff is counted: Virginia tile (flagged with the clause tag) beside Pennsylvania — enslaved people 347,000 vs 1,700, free people 539,000 vs 601,000 — and square counts land: Virginia 21, Pennsylvania 15. 'Those extra electoral votes gave the candidate from Virginia just enough to win' (transcript 5:24, on the 1800 election)." Why it works: a clause taught as a fraction is taught as a scoreboard. Evidence: ev/t302-three-fifths.jpg · ev/t320-va-pa-mid.jpg · ev/t322-va-pa-electors.jpg · Clip: clips/t19-elector-dots.mp4 (315–325 s).
- Style-library techniques-diagrams.md §T219: agrees verbatim (mechanics, window P12 @ 5:05–5:31, evidence, consumer). Tier DESCRIBED — OWED #101. No digest↔register disagreement.
- Transcript anchors (transcript.txt, sentence-timed): 295.59–305.78 the clause is named ("settled on something called the 'three-fifths clause'… count as 3/5 of a person") — the icon beat is the clause's tail, carried into this window. 305.78 "In 1800, Pennsylvania, a northern state, and Virginia, a southern one, had about the same number of free people living there." 312.96 "But Virginia was also home to hundreds of thousands of enslaved people… and ended up with more votes in the Electoral College than Pennsylvania." 324.17 "That year, those extra electoral votes gave the candidate from Virginia just enough to win."
- Packet gaps: Packet #12 adopted WITH GAPS — G3 (clip-filename offset) touches this item only through the clip name `t19-` serving packet-local T18; the digest entry's explicit citation resolves it. G4 (mechanics thinness) is cured for this item firsthand by the census below.

