127:### T18 — Three-fifths payoff counts (Virginia 21 vs Pennsylvania 15)
128-- **Timestamps:** 5:05–5:31
129-- **Mechanics:** A person icon carries the "Three-fifths clause" label; then the payoff is counted: Virginia tile (flagged with the clause tag) beside Pennsylvania — enslaved people 347,000 vs 1,700, free people 539,000 vs 601,000 — and square counts land: Virginia 21, Pennsylvania 15. "Those extra electoral votes gave the candidate from Virginia just enough to win" (transcript 5:24, on the 1800 election).
130-- **Why it works:** A clause usually taught as a fraction is taught as a scoreboard: two states, two populations, one count, one election decided. The abstract compromise is never left abstract.
131-- **Evidence:** ev/t302-three-fifths.jpg (clause icon + label, 302 s) · ev/t320-va-pa-mid.jpg (counts landing, 320 s) · ev/t322-va-pa-electors.jpg (21 vs 15 squares with population figures, 322 s) · Clip: clips/t19-elector-dots.mp4 (315–325 s)
132-
63:[ 295.59] As a compromise, they settled on something called the “three-fifths clause.“ It established that an enslaved person would only count as 3/5 of a person.
64:[ 305.78] In 1800, Pennsylvania, a northern state, and Virginia, a southern one, had about the same number of free people living there.
65:[ 312.96] But Virginia was also home to hundreds of thousands of enslaved people, who had no freedom, let alone a vote, and ended up with more votes in the Electoral College than Pennsylvania.
66:[ 324.17] That year, those extra electoral votes gave the candidate from Virginia just enough to win.
